    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3f837611-6cbb-5d63-9657-2bddeba37967",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Rewrite $5-3z+z^2$ in the form $(z+a)^2+b$. Find the signed numbers $a$ and $b$.\n\n$a=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$b=$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put the quadratic term first: $5-3z+z^2=z^2-3z+5$.\n\nThe half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{3}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^2-3z+5&=\\underbrace{z^2-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{9}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{9}{4}+5 \\\\\n&=\\left(z-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{9}{4}+5 \\\\\n&=\\left(z-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2+\\frac{11}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-3z$.\n\nComparing with $(z+a)^2+b$ gives $a=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $b=\\frac{11}{4}$.",
